le to  perform intermediate measurements  we think this concept of pseudorandom  generators for quantum space bounded  algorithms that read random bits is  interesting in its own right  one reason it's interesting is of course  the connection with eliminating  intermediate measurements that i already  described before  but we think it might be interesting  from other perspectives  for example  prgs provide an example of a  distinguishability task that small space  quantum algorithms cannot solve  so the problem of distinguishing a truly  uniformly random string from a pseudo  random string  is something that small space quantum  algorithms cannot solve and this is  interesting from a complexity theoretic  perspective  prgs for quantum algorithms reading  random bits are also interesting in the  setting  when the quantum algorithm cannot be  relied on doing what it's supposed to do  for example you could have a protocol  where the quantum algorithm needs to  toss a large number of random bits and  perform some operation based on them  suppose you cannot guarantee that the  quantum algorithm is indeed tossing  truly uniformly random bits  how do you ensure that the algorithm  behaves as it should behave  well if you cannot toss those random  bits yourself a pseudo random generator  might be a way for you to ensure that  the quantum algorithm is indeed behaving  as though it had tossed uniformly random  coins  another reason we think prgs are  interesting is that it quantifies how  much randomness is truly necessary to  simulate a quantum system  so these are settings where we think  prgs might find applications but of  course our main application is in the  problem of eliminating intermediate  measurements  so now let me talk a little bit about  the proofs of our results  first i start by  explaining this connection between prgs  and eliminating intermediate  measurements more clearly i already  described it but i am going to give more  detail now  so let us start with an algorithm queue  with t intermediate measurements how do  we differ these three intermediate  measurements in an efficient way  first using the equivalence between  measurements and randomness  we write down an equivalent quantum  algorithm q prime  that has no measurements  but uses t random bits  we then use our pseudo random generator  to replace these t uniformly random bits  by t pseudo random bits which contain  only s log t truly random bits  this incurs only a small blow up in the  running time and a small error  we then again use the equivalence  between measurements and randomness to  replace these s log t truly random bits  by s log t measurements  finally we defer these as log t  measurements  using s log p additional space  this is just the classical principle of  deferred measurements  so this is how using prgs we take an  algorithm queue with t intermediate  measurements and simulated by an  algorithm with an additional s log t  space with a small blow up in run time  and with no intermediate measurements  now let me describe some of the ideas  behind our construction of the pseudo  random generators  so here is our main lemma  given any quantum algorithm of time t  and space s which reads t random bits  we use b of r to denote the final state  of the algorithm on a random string r  let g be a variant of the inw generator  for space s time t classical algorithms  the seed length of such a generator is s  log t  we show that the average final state b  of r  when r is drawn uniformly at random from  0 1 to the capital t  is close to the average final state b of  g of r when r is drawn from the uniform  distribution over  0 1 to the little t and g is the inw  generator i just mentioned  so this is what we want to show  i am not going to describe the inw  generator but i am going to give you  some ideas behind the construction of  this inw generator  i begin by describing the  classical motivation behind the  construction of these inwa generator  and then show how it generalizes to the  quantum setting  the idea is as follows  we think of the algorithm b which is now  classical we think of it as a function  taking a uniformly random string y over  capital t bits and an initial state  and outputting a final state  so initially you have the all zero state  the algorithm reads y1 and updates the  memory the algorithm reads y2 and  updates the memory and so on  and finally the last state of the memory  is declared as the final state of the  algorithm  now consider the midpoint of this  algorithm and divide it into two equal  parts  so let b 0 be the first half of the  algorithm  and b 1 be the second half of the  algorithm  so now b 0 is an algorithm that takes a  uniformly random string y  over  t over two bits  and outputs a final state  this final state is defined to be the  initial state of b one the second half  so b one now takes a uniformly random  string y prime  over t over two bits that is independent  of y  here is the main observation  the only information that is passed from  b 0 to b 1 is the state at time t over 2  and this is a state on at most s bits  therefore the amount of information that  b0 could leak about the string y to the  program b1  is at most s bits  so one could hope that instead of using  y and y prime that are completely  independent of each other  we might be able to get away with using  y and y prime that are correlated by  means of an extractor  so here extractor is a suitable function  which i will describe later on  it acts on the string y and a few  additional random bits  and produces an output  the idea is that instead of using y  prime we would use the string extractor  applied to y comma d  where d is a uniformly random string  over a much smaller universe  for us d will actually be a string on  roughly s bits  so what this does is effectively  reducing the randomness from t to t over  2 plus roughly s  the idea behind the inwa generator is to  recurse on this construction  and intuitively recursing log t times  each time incurring a seed length of s  we would overall get a seed length of s  log t  so this is the intuition behind the  classical inw generator  to generalize this to the quantum  setting we make use of a quantum  extractor  so let me describe what a quantum  extractor is for our purposes  imagine a setting where alice has a  uniformly random string y  and bob has some information about  alice's input  so he is allowed to look at alice's  input and write down some information  so by this we mean a quantum state rho y  consisting of t qubits  so now alice's and bob's inputs are  correlated  now apply a quantum extractor on alice's  input  using some additional random seed s  so this modifies alice's input  and the property we have  of an extractor is that after the  application of the extractor alice's and  bob's inputs are nearly uncorrelated  so if you look at the final state of  alice's and bob's input after  application of the extractor  it looks almost like a tensor product  state  it behaves as though alice had a  uniformly random string y prime and bob  had a uniformly random rho y where y and  y prime are independent  so an extractor  injects few bits of randomness into  alice's input so that the resulting  input of alice is uncorrelated with  bob's input almost uncorrelated  so such objects have been studied  we make use of a  quantum extractor from the result of  fair and schaffner so they show that if  bob could store at most t qubits of  information about alice's input then you  only need to inject roughly t bits of  randomness into alice's input  so this is the quantum extractor that we  use to generalize the inw classical  pseudo random generator  so this completes an overview of the  proof  let me summarize  we show two main result  our first result is that quantum  algorithms with unitary operators and  intermediate measurements can be  simulated by algorithms with only  unitary operators and without  intermediate measurements  using very little overhead in time and  space  more precisely if our original algorithm  had time t and space s  then our new algorithm has time t poly s  and space s log t  in other words we give a version of the  principle of deferred measurements that  is both space efficient and time  efficient  our second result is that we show pseudo  random generators for space bounded  quantum algorithms that read classical  random bits  we show that a variant of the inw  pseudor and generator actually folds  such small space quantum algorithms  this brings me to the end of my talk  thanks for listening
 

